441: \nL442: POST/responses\nL443: Creates a model response. Provide cite315†text†platform.openai.com or cite316†image†platform.openai.com inputs to generate cite315†text†platform.openai.com or cite317†JSON†platform.openai.com outputs. Have the model call your own cite318†custom code†platform.openai.com or use built-in cite319†tools†platform.openai.com like cite320†web search†platform.openai.com or cite321†file search†platform.openai.com to use your own data as input for the model’s response.\nL444: ##### Parameters Expand Collapse\nL445: \nL446: --background: optional boolean\nL447: \nL448: Whether to run the model response in the background. cite322†Learn more†platform.openai.com .\nL449: \nL450: --context-management: optional array of object { type, compact_threshold }\nL451: \nL452: Context management configuration for this request.\nL453: \nL454: --conversation: optional string or cite323†ResponseConversationParam { id }\nL455: The conversation that this response belongs to. Items from this conversation are prepended to `input_items` for this response request. Input items and output items from this response are automatically added to this conversation after this response completes.\nL456: \nL457: --include: optional array of cite324†ResponseIncludable L458: \nL459: Specify additional output data to include in the model response. Currently supported values are:\nL460:   * `web_search_call.action.sources`: Include the sources of the web search tool call.\nL461:   * `code_interpreter_call.outputs`: Includes the outputs of python code execution in code interpreter tool call items.\nL462:   * `computer_call_output.output.image_url`: Include image urls from the computer call output.\nL463:   * `file_search_call.results`: Include the search results of the file search tool call.\nL465:   * `message.output_text.logprobs`: Include logprobs with assistant messages.\nL466:   * `reasoning.encrypted_content`: Includes an encrypted version of reasoning tokens in reasoning item outputs. This enables reasoning items to be used in multi-turn conversations when using the Responses API statelessly (like when the `store` parameter is set to `false`, or when an organization is enrolled in the zero data retention program).\nL467: --input: optional string or cite325†ResponseInput { easy_input_message, message, response_output_message, 29 more }\nL468: \nL469: Text, image, or file inputs to the model, used to generate a response.\nL470: \nL471: Learn more:\nL472: \nL473:   * cite315†Text inputs and outputs†platform.openai.com L474:   * cite316†Image inputs†platform.openai.com L475:   * cite326†File inputs†platform.openai.com L476:   * cite327†Conversation state†platform.openai.com L477:   * cite318†Function calling†platform.openai.com L478: \nL479: --instructions: optional string\nL480: A system (or developer) message inserted into the model’s context.\nL481: \nL482: When using along with `previous_response_id`, the instructions from a previous response will not be carried over to the next response. This makes it simple to swap out system (or developer) messages in new responses.\nL483: \nL484: --max-output-tokens: optional number\nL485: \nL486: An upper bound for the number of tokens that can be generated for a response, including visible output tokens and cite328†reasoning tokens†platform.openai.com .\nL487: --max-tool-calls: optional number\nL488: \nL489: The maximum number of total calls to built-in tools that can be processed in a response. This maximum number applies across all built-in tool calls, not per individual tool. Any further attempts to call a tool by the model will be ignored.\nL490: \nL491: --metadata: optional map[string]\nL492: Set of 16 key-value pairs that can be attached to an object. This can be useful for storing additional information about the object in a structured format, and querying for objects via API or the dashboard.\nL493: \nL494: Keys are strings with a maximum length of 64 characters. Values are strings with a maximum length of 512 characters.\nL495: \nL496: --model: optional string or cite329†ChatModel or \"o1-pro\" or \"o1-pro-2025-03-19\" or \"o3-pro\" or 16 more\nL497: Model ID used to generate the response, like `gpt-4o` or `o3`. OpenAI offers a wide range of models with different capabilities, performance characteristics, and price points. Refer to the cite330†model guide†platform.openai.com to browse and compare available models.\nL498: \nL499: --moderation: optional object { model, policy }\nL500: \nL501: Configuration for running moderation on the input and output of this response.\nL502: \nL503: --parallel-tool-calls: optional boolean\nL504: \nL505: Whether to allow the model to run tool calls in parallel.\nL506: --previous-response-id: optional string\nL507: \nL508: The unique ID of the previous response to the model. Use this to create multi-turn conversations. Learn more about cite327†conversation state†platform.openai.com . Cannot be used in conjunction with `conversation`.\nL509: \nL510: --prompt: optional object { id, variables, version }\nL511: \nL512: Reference to a prompt template and its variables. cite331†Learn more†platform.openai.com .\nL513: \nL514: --prompt-cache-key: optional string\nL515: Used by OpenAI to cache responses for similar requests to optimize your cache hit rates. Replaces the `user` field. cite332†Learn more†platform.openai.com .\nL516: \nL517: --prompt-cache-options: optional object { mode, ttl }\nL518: Options for prompt caching. Supported for `gpt-5.6` and later models. By default, OpenAI automatically chooses one implicit cache breakpoint. You can add explicit breakpoints to content blocks with `prompt_cache_breakpoint`. Each request can write up to four breakpoints. For cache matching, OpenAI considers up to the latest 80 breakpoints in the conversation, without a content-block lookback limit. Set `mode` to `explicit` to disable the implicit breakpoint.\nL582: An alternative to sampling with temperature, called nucleus sampling, where the model considers the results of the tokens with top_p probability mass. So 0.1 means only the tokens comprising the top 10% probability mass are considered.\nL583: \nL584: We generally recommend altering this or `temperature` but not both.\nL585: \nL586: Deprecated--truncation: optional \"auto\" or \"disabled\"\nL587: \nL588: The truncation strategy to use for the model response.\nL589:   * `auto`: If the input to this Response exceeds the model’s context window size, the model will truncate the response to fit the context window by dropping items from the beginning of the conversation.\nL590:   * `disabled` (default): If the input size will exceed the context window size for a model, the request will fail with a 400 error.\nL591: \nL592: Deprecated--user: optional string\nL593: This field is being replaced by `safety_identifier` and `prompt_cache_key`. Use `prompt_cache_key` instead to maintain caching optimizations. A stable identifier for your end-users. Used to boost cache hit rates by better bucketing similar requests and to help OpenAI detect and prevent abuse. cite334†Learn more†platform.openai.com .\nL594: ##### Returns Expand Collapse\nL595: \nL596: response: object { id, created_at, error, 31 more }\nL597: \nL598: id: string\nL599: \nL600: Unique identifier for this Response.\nL601: \nL602: created_at: number\nL603: \nL604: Unix timestamp (in seconds) of when this Response was created.\nL605: \nL606: error: object { code, message }\nL607: \nL608: An error object returned when the model fails to generate a Response.\nL609: \nL610: cite342†View schema details L611: \nL612: incomplete_details: object { reason }\nL613: \nL614: Details about why the response is incomplete.\nL615: \nL616: cite343†View schema details L617: instructions: string or array of cite344†ResponseInputItem L618: \nL619: A system (or developer) message inserted into the model’s context.\nL620: \nL6