ll make it more focused and deterministic. We generally recommend altering this or `top_p` but not both.\nL653: \nL654: tool_choice: cite337†ToolChoiceOptions or cite338†ToolChoiceAllowed { mode, tools, type } or cite339†ToolChoiceTypes { type } or 6 more\nL655: How the model should select which tool (or tools) to use when generating a response. See the `tools` parameter to see how to specify which tools the model can call.\nL656: \nL657: cite349†View schema details L658: \nL659: tools: array of cite340†Tool L660: \nL661: An array of tools the model may call while generating a response. You can specify which tool to use by setting the `tool_choice` parameter.\nL662: \nL663: We support the following categories of tools:\nL664:   * Built-in tools: Tools that are provided by OpenAI that extend the model’s capabilities, like cite320†web search†platform.openai.com or cite321†file search†platform.openai.com . Learn more about cite319†built-in tools†platform.openai.com .\nL665:   * MCP Tools: Integrations with third-party systems via custom MCP servers or predefined connectors such as Google Drive and SharePoint. Learn more about cite341†MCP Tools†platform.openai.com .\nL666:   * Function calls (custom tools): Functions that are defined by you, enabling the model to call your own code with strongly typed arguments and outputs. Learn more about cite318†function calling†platform.openai.com . You can also use custom tools to call your own code.\nL667: cite350†View schema details L668: \nL669: top_p: number\nL670: \nL671: An alternative to sampling with temperature, called nucleus sampling, where the model considers the results of the tokens with top_p probability mass. So 0.1 means only the tokens comprising the top 10% probability mass are considered.\nL672: \nL673: We generally recommend altering this or `temperature` but not both.\nL674: \nL675: background: optional boolean\nL676: \nL677: Whether to run the model response in the background.